# Verdict

**Open.** I cannot justify a rigorous Las Vegas \(L_N[\alpha,c]\) factoring algorithm with \(\alpha<2/5\) from the Cunningham representation alone.

**Proved, conditional on the internal chain.** Accepting [Theorem U, §§1–10](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/lv5unif_results.md), \(2/5\) remains available for every such \(N\). This is an internal, unrefereed dependency, not an independently established theorem. The published unconditional fallback is \(L_N[1/2,1+o(1)]\): H. W. Lenstra Jr.–C. Pomerance, “A rigorous time bound for factoring integers,” *JAMS* **5** (1992), 483–516. [Paper](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper85.pdf).

**Heuristic (size model).**

| Architecture | Exponent permitted by the model |
|---|---:|
| One special-\(q\) descent level, tiny-height \(f\) | \(2/5\) |
| Multiple levels in one field | \(1/3\) |
| Rational children only, choosing a special degree separately at each node | \(1/3\) |

Thus **Prop R2’s \(2/5\) barrier disappears when its counting premise is replaced by the actual SNFS supply of special polynomials**. The missing ingredients are analytic relation supply and, for the recursive Dixon architecture, fast recognition of arbitrary intermediate integers.

**Proved.** The literal request for *any* natural infinite special family has polynomial-time examples, including sufficiently close semiprimes (§4). Those do not establish an SNFS improvement.

# 1. Recomputed size model

**Proved—normalization.** A consistent pair is
\[
j=d\lceil e/d\rceil-e,\qquad
f_d(x)=x^d-sr^j,\qquad m_d=r^{\lceil e/d\rceil},
\]
so \(f_d(m_d)=r^jN\). Here \(0\le j<d\), hence
\[
\log H_f=O(d\log r+\log|s|),\qquad
\log m_d=\frac{\log N}{d}+O(\log r).
\]
The pair written in the question has a sign mismatch in \(j\). The needed degrees satisfy \(d=o(e)\), which ensures small height; the assertion for *every* \(d\le\log N\) does not uniformly ensure \(N^{o(1)}\) height.

**Proved.** Available representations need not define irreducible degree-\(d\) fields. A clean family avoiding this issue is \(N=r^e-s\), where \(s\) is a fixed prime coprime to \(r\): every displayed \(f_d\) is Eisenstein at \(s\).

Put
\[
t=\log N,\quad \lambda=\log t,\quad D=t/\lambda,\quad
E=\log B\asymp D^\alpha\lambda.
\]
Let \(U\asymp E/\lambda\) solve \(U\log U\asymp E\), and write
\[
T=EU\asymp D^{2\alpha}\lambda.
\]

**Heuristic (size model).** Random-integer smoothness allows a value of logarithmic size \(O(T)\) to be \(B\)-smooth at cost \(\exp(O(E))\). Merely assuming \(H_f=N^{o(1)}\) is insufficient: one needs \(\log H_f=O(T)\), with suitable constants. The explicit SNFS heights above comfortably satisfy this at the degrees used below.

## One level

**Heuristic (size model).** Retain the project’s split:
\[
k=O(D^\alpha),\quad
\log q\simeq t/(2k),\quad
\nu=1/(4k),\quad V=\sqrt q\,S,\quad \log S=O(E).
\]
For \(G(a,b)=a-mb\), typical sizes are
\[
\begin{aligned}
\log|G(a,b)/q|&\simeq t/d-\nu t+\log S,\\
\log|F(a,b)|&\simeq \log H_f+d\nu t+d\log S.
\end{aligned}
\]
Consequently, ignoring the explicitly displayed search-width term,
\[
\theta_{\rm SNFS}
=\frac1d+\frac{\log H_f}{t}+\frac{d-1}{4k}.
\]

**Proved—arithmetic within this model.** The original randomized-design floors on \(h\) do not apply to a fixed polynomial. Nevertheless,
\[
\min_d\left(\frac1d+\frac{d-1}{4k}\right)
=\frac1{\sqrt k}+O(k^{-1}).
\]
Smoothness requires \(\theta=O(D^{2\alpha-1})\), while split acceptance requires \(k=O(D^\alpha)\). Therefore
\[
D^{-\alpha/2}\ll D^{2\alpha-1},
\qquad \alpha\ge2/5.
\]
Equality uses \(d\asymp D^{1/5}\), \(k\asymp D^{2/5}\). Here \(d\log S=o(T)\).

**Heuristic (size model).** Tiny coefficients improve constants and remove particular proof-design costs; they do not change this one-level exponent.

## Multiple levels in one field

**Heuristic (size model).** At a target \(q\), with \(z=\log q\), the sizes become
\[
X_F\simeq \log H_f+\frac d2z+d\log S,\qquad
X_r\simeq t/d-z/2+\log S.
\]
At the last level, smoothness to \(B\) requires approximately
\[
t/d\lesssim T,\qquad dE\lesssim T.
\]
Thus
\[
D^{1-2\alpha}\lesssim d\lesssim D^\alpha,
\]
giving the model floor \(1/3\).

**Heuristic (size model).** At \(\alpha=1/3\), choose \(d\asymp D^{1/3}\), retain \(d\log S\), and contract target logarithms by a fixed factor. As in recurse §3.4, \(O(\log\log N)\) levels and \(\exp(O((\log\log N)^2))\) nodes permit a finite \(L[1/3]\) constant. Algebraic children still require the project’s unresolved endogenous-child supply estimates.

## Prop R2 without (M1): variable degree, rational children only

**Heuristic (size model).** Require every algebraic value to be \(B\)-smooth, but allow rational children bounded by \(e^y\). At a node of target size \(z\ge E\), feasibility becomes
\[
d(z+E)\lesssim T,\qquad
\frac{t/d-z/2+O(E)}{y}\lesssim U.
\]
Choosing
\[
d(z)\asymp \frac{T}{z+E}
\]
gives
\[
\frac yz\asymp\frac{t}{EU^2}
\asymp D^{1-3\alpha},
\]
up to adjustable constants.

**Proved—arithmetic within this model.** Below \(1/3\), contraction is impossible: \(d\lesssim T/z\) forces rational logarithmic size \(\gtrsim tz/T\), hence \(y/z\gtrsim t/(TU)\to\infty\). At \(1/3\), constants can permit \(y\le\eta z\), \(\eta<1\). Degrees increase from \(O(1)\) near the top to \(O(D^{1/3})\) near the leaves. **The former forbidden window disappears.**

**Open—algorithmic qualification.** Recurse §3.1 assumes an algorithm applicable to *every* smaller integer. Cunningham numbers are not closed under its shape tests: the integers \(u_0,v_0\) are arbitrary. Calling Theorem U on \(\sqrt N\)-sized test inputs still costs exponent \(2/5\). Thus the model’s \(1/3\) needs a separate recognition argument. Conventional SNFS relation collection avoids this particular recursive requirement.

# 2. Which randomness mechanisms survive?

**Proved—dependency distinction.** Kern is an algebraic fibre-counting lemma for a full coefficient box. GM is conditional smoothness at a good modulus, obtained by coefficient averaging. Theorem R needs the unrestricted two-dimensional rational-side distribution. Lemma W needs an independent multiplier with sufficiently spread residues. FM and LD* assemble these inputs. Changing coordinates does not automatically reproduce their probability laws.

| Device | Status and surviving ingredients |
|---|---|
| **(a) Random rational side sharing a fixed root** | **Proved:** permissible \((g_0,g_1)\) lie in a determinant-\(N\) lattice, rather than the project’s full square. Theorem R therefore does not transfer. An unrestricted BLP base-\((m_1,m_2)\) choice restores its rational distribution, but generally requires rebuilding \(f\). FM/LD* additionally need their coefficient and multiplier laws. A small multiplicative root twist gives an isomorphic field. |
| **(b) Multipliers \(wN\)** | **Proved:** \(wf\) retains the same field after removing content. For prime \(w>B\), every nonzero value of \(wF\) is nonsmooth, so this alone supplies no relations. Lemma W’s residue bound can remain true but is insufficient. Adding \(GR_c\) can create new fields and restore Kern/conditional GM; with an independent suitably large \(w\), it can also restore W. Global FM/LD* still require the rational-distribution argument. |
| **(c) Translation, scaling, Möbius changes** | **Proved:** \(f(x+t)\), \(u^df(x/u)\), and invertible rational Möbius transformations define isomorphic fields. They change the sampled region or coordinates, not the underlying field. None creates the missing coefficient-box law. Existing lemmas transfer only when their complete sampling hypotheses transfer. |
| **(d) Several special polynomials/representations** | **Open:** averaging over the explicit \((d,j)\) choices does not provide known FM/LD*/W estimates. The standard list has only polynomially many choices in \(\log N\). This cardinality is not an impossibility proof: one good fixed field could suffice. |
| **(e) Random special-\(q\) only** | **Proved:** lattice geometry and the \(\ell\)-averaged analytic inputs remain available. They do not replace coefficient smoothing, rational-side averaging, or residue spreading. **Open:** the requisite first and second moments for a fixed SNFS pair. |
| **(f) \(f+cg\)** | **Proved:** a full independent coefficient box restores Kern and, under the height/window/good-modulus hypotheses, GM. The resultant remains fixed, so W is absent and LD* does not follow. Fixed \(g\) still lacks Theorem R; the full FM conclusion is not inherited. |

**Proved—height cost in (f).** For \(g=x-m\), \(m>1\), and nonzero \(c\),
\[
\|(x-m)c\|_\infty\ge(m-1)\|c\|_\infty.
\]
Choose a coefficient of \(c\) of maximal absolute value and inspect the corresponding coefficient of \((x-m)c\). Therefore
\[
\|f+(x-m)c\|_\infty
\ge(m-1)\|c\|_\infty-\|f\|_\infty.
\]
Even unit perturbations generally introduce height on the \(N^{1/d}\) scale; Kern’s uniform probability estimate needs the additional box margin \(H\gtrsim dV^2\).

**Proved.** Reinstating the complete original law restores the original internal results and their design floors. Small height of an initial special polynomial does not remove the size of the eventual randomized polynomial.

# 3. A rigorous uniqueness statement

**Proved—resultant separation.** Suppose \(f,h\in\mathbb Z[x]\), of degrees \(d,q\), share a specified root modulo \(N\). If they are coprime over \(\mathbb Q\), then
\[
N\le|\operatorname{Res}(f,h)|
\le \|f\|_2^{\,q}\|h\|_2^{\,d}.
\]
The first inequality follows from the integral resultant identity evaluated at the common root. The second is Hadamard’s inequality for the Sylvester matrix.

Consequently,
\[
H_h\ge
\frac{N^{1/d}}
{(d+1)^{q/(2d)}(q+1)^{1/2}H_f^{q/d}}.
\]

**Proved.** In particular, if \(f\) is irreducible, \(\deg h\le d\), and
\[
((d+1)H_fH_h)^d<N,
\]
then \(h\) is a rational scalar multiple of \(f\). For SNFS heights with \(\log H_f=o(t/d)\), this gives a genuine \(N^{(1-o(1))/d}\) separation threshold.

**Proved—scope.** This establishes uniqueness at a **fixed modular root and bounded degree**. Different special degrees normally use different modular roots, so there is no contradiction with their availability. It is not a universal lower bound against all field-randomization constructions.

**Proved—rational analogue.** If \(g_0\equiv mg_1\pmod N\), \(|g_i|\le M\), and \((m+1)M<N\), then \(mg_1-g_0=0\). Primitive pairs are therefore just \(\pm(m,1)\). This directly explains the failure of small unrestricted rational-side randomization at the original root.

# 4. Special families with rigorous advantages

**Proved—cyclotomic decomposition.**
\[
r^e-1=\prod_{a\mid e}\Phi_a(r).
\]
These are efficiently computable integer factors, not necessarily prime factors. Möbius inversion gives
\[
\log\Phi_e(r)=\varphi(e)\log r+O_r(1):
\]
the error is bounded by the convergent series
\(\sum_{a\ge1}|\log(1-r^{-a})|\).

Since \(\varphi(e)=e^{1-o(1)}\), the primitive component retains logarithmic size \((\log N)^{1-o(1)}\). For \(r^e+1\), the corresponding component is \(\Phi_{2e}(r)\). Thus cyclotomic decomposition alone does not improve the \(L\)-exponent for complete factorization.

**Literature.** Aurifeuillian identities provide further explicit factors at suitable evaluations; standard fixed-number splittings improve sizes and constants without supplying a general smaller exponent for the remaining prime factorization. R. P. Brent, “On computing factors of cyclotomic polynomials,” *Math. Comp.* **61** (1993), 131–149. [Paper and abstract](https://maths-people.anu.edu.au/brent/pub/pub135.html).

**Proved—an infinite polynomial-time family.** Consider odd semiprimes \(N=pq\) with
\[
0<q-p\le(\log p)^2.
\]
There are infinitely many: otherwise prime gaps eventually exceeding \((\log p)^2\) would contradict the prime number theorem. For sufficiently large members,
\[
\left\lceil\sqrt N\right\rceil=(p+q)/2,
\]
because
\[
(p+q)/2-\sqrt{pq}
=\frac{(q-p)^2}{4((p+q)/2+\sqrt{pq})}<1.
\]
One Fermat square test recovers \(p,q\). This is polynomial time, hence exponent \(0\), and supplies complete factorization on this family.

**Proved—group-structure promise.** Squarefree integers for which every \(p\mid N\) has \(p-1\) \(B\)-smooth can also be completely factored in expected \(B(\log N)^{O(1)}\) time: construct a known multiple
\[
K=\prod_{\ell\le B}\ell^{\lfloor\log N/\log\ell\rfloor}
\]
of every \(p-1\), then use random powering and successive-square gcd splitting. This yields any specified sub-\(2/5\) exponent when \(B=L_N[\beta]\), \(\beta<2/5\). The promise concerns factor-group orders; a Cunningham representation does not imply it.

# 5. Literature audit

**Literature—SNFS is heuristic.** A. K. Lenstra, H. W. Lenstra Jr., M. S. Manasse and J. M. Pollard, “The number field sieve,” *STOC 1990*, 564–572; expanded in *The Development of the Number Field Sieve*, LNM **1554** (1993), 11–42. The special-form analysis gives the heuristic constant \((32/9)^{1/3}\), not a rigorous Las Vegas runtime theorem. J. P. Buhler–H. W. Lenstra Jr.–C. Pomerance, “Factoring integers with the number field sieve,” same volume, 50–94, gives the general construction and heuristic analysis. [Original volume](https://www.cs.umd.edu/~gasarch/TOPICS/factoring/1993_Book_TheDevelopmentOfTheNumberField.pdf).

**Literature—Semaev’s special primes.** I. A. Semaev, “Special prime numbers and discrete logs in finite prime fields,” *Math. Comp.* **71** (2002), 363–377, DOI **10.1090/S0025-5718-00-01308-9**. It develops resultant constructions for special primes, including bounded-coefficient exponential forms. The stated \(L_p[1/3,(32/9)^{1/3}]\) expected runtime is explicitly **heuristic**. Its algebraic identities do not establish the needed smoothness distributions. [Article text](https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=15fa3b43f5c70796caa135ee088cffa43a2ef497&repid=rep1&type=pdf).

**Literature—Gordon.** D. M. Gordon, “Discrete logarithms in \(GF(p)\) using the number field sieve,” *SIAM J. Discrete Math.* **6** (1993), 124–138, DOI **10.1137/0406010**, states heuristic expected complexity \(L_p[1/3,3^{2/3}]\). It is not a rigorous sub-\(1/2\) prime-field DLP result. [Article](https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/0406010).

**Literature—Joux–Lercier.** A. Joux–R. Lercier, “Improvements to the general number field sieve for discrete logarithms in prime fields. A comparison with the Gaussian integer method,” *Math. Comp.* **72** (2003), 953–967, DOI **10.1090/S0025-5718-02-01482-5**. It improves individual-logarithm descent and demonstrates computations; it does not prove the required fixed-polynomial relation-supply theorem. [Paper](https://cr.yp.to/bib/2003/joux.pdf).

**Literature—rigorous \(1/3\), with a crucial qualification.** J. D. Lee–R. Venkatesan, “Rigorous analysis of a randomised number field sieve,” *J. Number Theory* **187** (2018), 92–159, DOI **10.1016/j.jnt.2017.10.019**:

- Theorem 2.1 proves randomized generation of potentially trivial square congruences at \(L[1/3,(64/9)^{1/3}]\).
- Theorem 2.3 obtains factoring for \(N=pq\), \(p\equiv q\equiv3\pmod4\), assuming their character-decorrelation Conjecture 7.1.
- This uses large polynomial randomization and does not establish fixed-pair SNFS or its smaller constant.

[Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873).

**Literature.** C. Pomerance, “Fast, rigorous factorization and discrete logarithm algorithms,” in *Discrete Algorithms and Complexity*, Academic Press (1987), 119–143, gives rigorous \(L[1/2,O(1)]\) algorithms, including prime-field DLP. [Paper](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/disclog.pdf).

**Open—search conclusion.** I found no established rigorous sub-\(1/2\) SNFS runtime theorem for Cunningham residuals, or special-representation prime-field DLP theorem resolving the large prime-order component at \(L[1/3]\). This is a literature-search conclusion, not an impossibility theorem.

# 6. A sufficient missing statement, and existing friability results

**Conjectured—direct SNFS supply.** Fix an explicit irreducible special pair, for example the Eisenstein pairs above. Let
\[
d\asymp D^{1/3},\qquad B=\exp(b\Lambda_{1/3}),\qquad
A=\exp(a\Lambda_{1/3}),
\]
and let \(\Omega\) be an appropriate primitive box of radius \(A\). A sufficient analytic input for relation collection is a uniform lower bound
\[
\#\{P\in\Omega:
P^+(|G(P)|)\le B,\ P^+(|F(P)|)\le B\}
\ge |\Omega|\rho(X_r,B)\rho(X_F,B)e^{-o(\Lambda_{1/3})},
\]
with actual norm envelopes, local exclusions, and constants permitting more relations than columns.

**Open.** Together with an adequately bounded probability of a nontrivial square congruence and controlled post-processing, this would give a rigorous Las Vegas \(L[1/3]\) algorithm. Relation counting alone does not prove nontriviality.

**Conjectured—project-compatible alternative.** For one-level descent, sufficient fixed-pair estimates are
\[
\mathbb E_\ell N_\ell\ge m(\log N)^{-C},\qquad
\mathbb E_\ell N_\ell^2\le(\log N)^C(m+m^2),
\quad m=S^2\rho_r\rho_F.
\]
These yield a polylogarithmic density of descendable primes. Below \(2/5\), analogous estimates must hold under the actual conditional target laws throughout descent; arbitrary density alone cannot suffice, by Prop SI. The recursive architecture additionally needs the recognition issue identified in §1.

**Literature—general binary forms.** A. Balog–V. Blomer–C. Dartyge–G. Tenenbaum, “Friable values of binary forms,” *Comment. Math. Helv.* **87** (2012), 639–667, DOI **10.4171/CMH/264**, proves positive-proportion results at power-type thresholds: \(y=x^{g-2+\varepsilon}\) generally, and \(y=x^{1/\sqrt e+\varepsilon}\) for cubics. These are far from the required growing-degree two-sided estimates. [Paper](https://doi.org/10.4171/CMH/264).

**Literature—important exception.** R. Barbulescu–A. Lachand, “Some mathematical remarks on the polynomial selection in NFS,” *Math. Comp.* **86** (2017), 397–418, DOI **10.1090/mcom/3112**, Theorem 4.2, proves substantially stronger asymptotics for fixed imaginary quadratic forms of fundamental discriminant, reaching \(y\ge\exp((\log\log X)^{5/3+\varepsilon})\). Thus “all known binary-form results require power-type smoothness” would be false. The theorem does not provide growing-degree uniformity, special-\(q\) restrictions, or simultaneous smoothness with the large rational form. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1403.0184).

**Literature.** A. Mounier, “Un crible minorant effectif pour les entiers friables,” *Acta Arith.* **220** (2025), 121–159, DOI **10.4064/aa240514-8-4**, supplies effective lower bounds from distribution information; its general higher-degree binary-form applications retain power-type thresholds. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.13198).

**Open.** Small SNFS norms therefore remove a model obstruction without supplying the required distribution theorem. The decisive unresolved statement is uniform smoothness of the actual sparse, correlated fixed-form values—not simply smoothness of integers of the same size.
