   122	### 1.1 Lemma RES and the coefficient floor [proved]
   123	
   124	**Lemma RES.** Let f, g ∈ Z[x], deg f = d ≥ 1, deg g = δ ≥ 1, coprime in Q[x], and f(m) ≡ g(m) ≡ 0 (mod N) for some
   125	integer m. Then N | Res(f, g), Res(f, g) ≠ 0, and N ≤ |Res(f, g)| ≤ ‖f‖₂^δ‖g‖₂^d.
   126	
   127	*Proof.* The adjugate of the Sylvester matrix gives A, B ∈ Z[x] with A f + B g = Res(f, g) (e.g. Lang, *Algebra*,
   128	IV §8). Evaluating at m gives Res ≡ 0 (mod N). Res ≠ 0 because f and g have no common root in Q̄. Hadamard's
   129	inequality applied to the rows of the Sylvester matrix (δ shifted copies of f's coefficient vector and d of g's) gives
   130	|Res| ≤ ‖f‖₂^δ‖g‖₂^d. ∎
   131	
   132	**Corollary (linear side).** If g = g₁x − g₀ and f is irreducible of degree d ≥ 2, then ‖f‖₂‖g‖₂^d ≥ N. With
   133	a := log‖f‖₂ and b := log‖g‖₂ we get a + db ≥ t, hence a + b ≥ t/d + a(1 − 1/d) ≥ t/d. So **θ_coeff :=
   134	(log‖f‖ + log‖g‖)/t ≥ 1/d − O(log d/t)** for every NFS pair with a linear side. SNFS attains equality up to
   135	O(d log r/t).
   136	
   137	`rigidity.check_res_floor` checks the divisibility and the Hadamard bound exactly on SNFS pairs (tests).
