   227	### 1.5 Recognition: Prop REC [proved accounting, given the tools]
   228	
   229	In the Dixon-split architecture, each accepted row requires the complete factorisation (equivalently, the shape
   230	certificate) of the Gauss-reduced halves u₀, v₀ of a random square. These are arbitrary integers ≤ √(2N), with all
   231	prime factors in the target set ⊂ (Y, 2Y], Y = N^{1/(2k)} = L_N[1 − α]. Every intermediate node of a multi-level tree
   232	similarly has to factor rational cofactors with prime factors up to L[1 − α].
   233	
   234	**Prop REC.** Let α < 2/5, and suppose the algorithm uses only these rigorous recognisers: (a) a recursive call of
   235	a general factoring algorithm, of which the best rigorous one available is Theorem U; (b) LPP's hyperelliptic method
   236	(HECM); (c) trial division, Pollard–Strassen and batch smoothness. Then the expected cost per accepted row is not
   237	bounded by L_N[α]^{O(1)} through any of them, so these recognisers cannot certify the split within L[α].
   238	- (a) costs L_{√(2N)}[2/5, c_U] = L_N[2/5, 2^{−2/5}c_U + o(1)] ≫ L_N[α]^{O(1)}.
   239	- (b) costs L_Y[2/3]^{O(1)} = L_N[2(1 − α)/3]^{O(1)}, and 2(1 − α)/3 > α ⟺ α < 2/5 (lemmaM (c)).
   240	- (c) costs Y^{Ω(1)} or π(2Y)^{Ω(1)} = L_N[1 − α]^{Ω(1)}.
   241	- Restricting the target set does not help (Lemma S″). Any target set G ⊂ J of relative density δ multiplies the
   242	  acceptance by about δ^{2k}, and k ≍ D^α. So G must have density (log N)^{−O(1)}. The primes p ∈ J with p − 1
   243	  L[α]-smooth (Pollard p − 1) have density exp(−(1 + o(1))u log u) with u ≍ D^{1−2α}. Then δ^{2k} = exp(−Θ(D^{1−α} log D)),
   244	  which is not L[α]^{O(1)}.
   245	
   246	*Proof.* (a) is Theorem U at √(2N) with L_{√x}[α, c] = L_x[α, 2^{−α}c + o(1)] ([rec] §3.1). (b) is LPP's bound with
   247	y = 2Y. (c) is direct. ∎
   248	
   249	So in the general case recursion is what removes the recognition cost ([rec] §3.1). It cannot be used inside a
   250	special family, because u₀ and v₀ are not special (codex snfsrig_1 §1, "algorithmic qualification"). A rigorous
   251	recogniser at ECM speed, L_p[1/2, O(1)], for all p in a target set of polylogarithmic density would cost L_N[(1 − α)/2]
   252	and suffice down to α = 1/3. What is known is in §5.
   253	
   254	---
   255	
   256	## 2. Recovering randomness: what survives
   257	
   258	The relation-supply proofs use field randomness in exactly four places ([U] §§4–6, [ld], thmA §1):
   259	- (R) **Theorem R / FIB**: for fixed P, G_g(P) = g₁a − g₀b is near-uniform on an interval as g ranges over the 2D box
   260	  𝒢 = [M/2, M]². This gives rational smoothness.
   261	- (GM) **GM-U, one-point smoothing**: for fixed P, F_A(P) = F̂(P) + G(P)⟨c, v(P)⟩ ranges over an AP mod G(P) as c
   262	  ranges over [−H, H]^d. This gives algebraic smoothness via Lemma 8.5′.
   263	- (K) **Kern-U**: for P ≠ P′, (F_A(P), F_A(P′)) is near-uniform over c (dimension-free fibre count). This is LD*'s
   264	  Step 1.
   265	- (W) **Lemma W**: F̂(g₀, g₁) = Nw with w a random prime spreads the forced residue F_A(P) ≡ wN(b/g₁)^d mod G(P).
   266	  This is LD*'s Step 4.
   267	
   268	### 2.1 Lemma HR (height rigidity) [proved]
   269	
   270	Let f₀ ∈ Z[x] be irreducible of degree d with f₀(m) ≡ 0 (mod N). Let f ∈ Z[x] have degree δ ≥ 1 with f(m) ≡ 0
   271	(mod N), and suppose f₀ ∤ f in Q[x]. Then ‖f‖₂ ≥ (N/‖f₀‖₂^δ)^{1/d}.
   272	
   273	*Proof.* f₀ is irreducible and f₀ ∤ f, so f₀ and f are coprime. Apply Lemma RES. ∎
   274	
   275	For N ∈ 𝔈 and f₀ = f_d, with δ ≤ d: ‖f‖₂ ≥ N^{1/d}/(√2·r^{d}|s|). So **any field with the special root other than
   276	K_d itself has height ≥ N^{(1−o(1))/d}**, as long as d² log r = o(t). The algebraic values of such a field have size
   277	≥ N^{1/d}V^d. Codex adds the scope: this is uniqueness at a fixed root and bounded degree. Other special degrees use
   278	other roots m_{d′}, and they *are* available. They are the O(D²) fields of the special family, and Prop RB covers them.
   279	`rigidity.exhaustive_small_height` enumerates all f of degree ≤ 3 and height ≤ 3 with f(m) ≡ 0 for N = 2^{30} − 3 and
   280	finds only ±f₀. For N = 509, where HR guarantees nothing, non-multiples appear, all consistent with the bound.
   281	
   282	### 2.2 Lemma PH (perturbation height) [proved; codex snfsrig_1 §2]
   283	
   284	For m > 1 and 0 ≠ c ∈ Z[x], ‖(x − m)c‖_∞ ≥ (m − 1)‖c‖_∞. Hence ‖f₀ + (x − m)c‖_∞ ≥ (m − 1)‖c‖_∞ − ‖f₀‖_∞.
   285	
   286	*Proof.* Pick i with |c_i| = ‖c‖_∞. The x^i-coefficient of (x − m)c is c_{i−1} − mc_i (with c_{−1} := 0), and
   287	|c_{i−1} − mc_i| ≥ m|c_i| − |c_{i−1}| ≥ (m − 1)‖c‖_∞. ∎
   288	
   289	So GM/Kern-type perturbations of the special field cost height ≈ H·N^{1/d} at once. At H = 1 the algebraic norm is
   290	already that of a GNFS pair with rational side N^{1/d}, and (M1) holds: (d + 1)t/d + 2t/d ≥ t.
   291	
   292	### 2.3 Lemma RS (rational-side rigidity) [proved]
   293	
   294	Let 0 ≤ m < N, M ≥ 1 with (m + 1)M < N, and let (g₀, g₁) ∈ Z² satisfy g₀ ≡ mg₁ (mod N) and |g₀|, |g₁| ≤ M.
   295	Then g₀ = mg₁. So the only primitive pairs are ±(m, 1), and G_g(P) = ±(a − bm).
   296	
   297	*Proof.* |mg₁ − g₀| ≤ (m + 1)M < N, and N divides it. ∎
   298	
   299	So the 2D-random rational side (R) with the special root needs M ≥ N/(m + 1) ≈ N^{1−1/d}, which gives rational values
   300	≥ N^{1−1/d}V/ℓ and θ ≥ 1 − 1/d. A random root m′ (BLP base-(m₁, m₂)) restores (R) but discards f₀: that is GNFS. For
   301	Cunningham N | r^e − 1 with d | e the other roots mζ^i (ζ = r^{e/d}) are known, but they are generic residues
   302	mod N. Their root lattices have shortest vectors ≍ √N by Minkowski, so this does not help either (remark, not
   303	needed).
   304	
   305	### 2.4 Prop DI (device invariance) [proved]
   306	
   307	Let F₀(a, b) := b^d f₀(a/b) and G₀(a, b) := a − mb. For each device below there are T ∈ M₂(Z) with det T ≠ 0 and
   308	c₁, c₂ ∈ Q^× such that the device's pair (F, G) satisfies (F, G)(P) = (c₁F₀(TP), c₂G₀(TP)) for all P ∈ Z², and the
   309	device's field is isomorphic to Q(α₀) compatibly with ψ:
   310	
   311	| device | T | c₁, c₂ |
   312	|---|---|---|
   313	| multiplier N·w, kN (f = w f₀, the same root) | id | w, 1 |
   314	| root twist λ (root λm, f = λ^d f₀(x/λ)) | diag(1, λ) | 1, 1 |
   315	| shift f₀(x + t) (root m − t) | [[1, t], [0, 1]] | 1, 1 |
   316	| scaling u^d f₀(x/u) (root um) | diag(1, u) | 1, 1 |
   317	| Möbius (γx + δ)^d f₀((αx + β)/(γx + δ)) | [[α, β], [γ, δ]] | 1, (by G₀∘T) |
   318	| N r^i = m′^d − s r^{i′} (other j, same d) | diag(1, r^{i}) | r-power, 1 |
   319	
   320	*Proof.* Each identity is the substitution x = TP in the homogenised forms (exact symbolic checks:
   321	`rigidity.device_identities`). The fields are Q(α₀) with the corresponding Möbius image of α₀. ∎
   322	
   323	So the value set of a device on a box Ω is the value set of the fixed pair (F₀, G₀) on TΩ ⊂ Z². For a prime w > B,
   324	every nonzero value of wF₀ is non-smooth; after removing content nothing changes (codex). Hence N·w gives no new
   325	randomness, and Lemma W has nothing to act on. A small-coefficient polynomial for r^e − s times w in a *new* field
   326	would need a new known root; by HR it cannot share m.
   327	
   328	### 2.5 Prop RB (relativisation barrier for fixed field families) [proved]
   329	
   330	**Setting.** A one-level design 𝔇 is given by Φ fixed pairs (f_i, g_i) (devices allowed) and the special-q targets
   331	ℓ ∈ J. Each target is searched in a box of ≤ W points of its lattice; [U] has W ≤ 400S². For a set 𝒮 ⊂ Z let
   332	γ_𝔇(𝒮) be the descendability density of [U] §1 computed with 𝒮 in place of the B-smooth integers 𝒮_B. Let 𝒱 be
   333	the set of all values G_i(P)/ℓ over all (i, ℓ, P). Then |𝒱| ≤ Φπ_JW.
   334	
   335	**Prop RB.** (i) γ_𝔇(𝒮_B ∖ 𝒱) = 0.
   336	(ii) Every input of the form "#(𝒮 ∩ I) is bounded above by …" (DS-U, L2_fam, Rankin, Brun–Titchmarsh-type) holds for
   337	𝒮′ := 𝒮_B ∖ 𝒱 because 𝒮′ ⊂ 𝒮_B.
   338	(iii) Every input of the form "Σ_{I∈ℐ}|#(𝒮 ∩ I) − main(I)| ≤ err" (HT local estimates, Lemma U1, Lemma 8.5′(R′),
   339	Tenenbaum's count in APs) holds for 𝒮′ with err + |𝒱|·|ℐ|.
   340	
   341	Hence a proof of γ_𝔇 > 0 that uses only inputs of type (ii), and inputs of type (iii) whose error budgets exceed
   342	|𝒱|·|ℐ|, would also prove γ_𝔇(𝒮′) > 0, contradicting (i). Such a proof therefore cannot exist. In the SNFS regime the
   343	budgets do exceed |𝒱|·|ℐ| (next paragraph).
   344	
   345	*Proof.* (i): every success requires some G_i(P)/ℓ ∈ 𝒮, and 𝒱 ∩ 𝒮′ = ∅. (ii): monotonicity. (iii): for each I,
   346	|#(𝒮_B ∩ I) − #(𝒮′ ∩ I)| ≤ |𝒱|. ∎
   347	
   348	**The SNFS numbers [proved arithmetic].**
   349	- *Size of 𝒱.* Φ = (log N)^{O(1)} (the fields K_d, d ≤ D, together with devices whose T have entries
   350	  (log N)^{O(1)}). Also π_J = N^{2ν+o(1)} and W = L^{O(1)}, so |𝒱| = N^{2ν+o(1)}.
   351	- *Size of the counted values.* The rational values have size X_r = N^{1/d−ν+o(1)}.
   352	- *Inputs on intervals or single APs.* These count sets I of integers of size x ≥ X_r with expected size
   353	  ≥ xρ/B^{O(1)} and budget ε·(main), ε = L^{−O(1)}. Their deletion error |𝒱| is negligible because 1/d − ν > 2ν.
   354	- *Barban–Davenport–Halberstam / Bombieri–Vinogradov-type sums* (Lemma 8.5′). These range over |ℐ| ≤ x^{1/2} classes,
   355	  with budget ≍ ε·Ψ(x). The deletion error is ≤ |𝒱|·x^{1/2}, which is ≤ ε·xρ iff |𝒱| ≤ ερx^{1/2}. At x ≥ X_r this holds
   356	  iff 2ν < (1/d − ν)/2, i.e. **5ν < 1/d (d < 4k/5)**.
   357	- *Regime.* This holds throughout the regime that matters: at the 2/5 design d ≍ D^{1/5} and ν ≍ D^{−2/5}, and for every
   358	  fixed d once k > 5d/4. Algebraic values are larger still.
   359	
   360	*Contrast with Theorem U [consistency check].* There the union over the field law of the values F_A(P) is an AP of
   361	length ≍ HV^{d−1}. The point masses are ≤ 1/((2H + 1)V′^{d−1}) ([U] Lemma SM1). Deleting K values changes FM by
   362	≤ K·#𝒫·max mass, which is negligible for any K = N^{O(ν)}. So the same statistical inputs do suffice. The barrier is
   363	the small support of a fixed family, exactly Prop SI(i)/(ii) of [nest] but for the plain smooth set.
   364	
   365	**What a proof would need instead.** It would need non-relativising, form-specific information: arithmetic of the
   366	specific values (Norm(a − bα₀) in a Kummer field, a − b·r^j in base r), such as type I/II sums for incomplete norm forms
   367	(Heath-Brown, Maynard), BBDT's methods for binary forms, or prescribed-digit methods (Bourgain). §4–§5 compare the
   368	required ranges with what these give.
   369	
   370	### 2.6 Device table (what survives)
   371	
   372	"✗" = the ingredient's hypotheses fail. "✓" = it holds, but only by giving up the SNFS norms.
   373	
   374	| device | (R) Theorem R | (GM) | (K) Kern | (W) | FM | LD* | keeps SNFS norms? |
   375	|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   376	| (a) random rational side, special root | ✗ (RS: only ±(x − m) below N^{1−1/d}) | — | — | — | ✗ | ✗ | yes, but then fixed g |
   377	| (a′) random root m′ (BLP) | ✓ | needs a new f | — | — | GNFS | GNFS | **no** (base-m′ heights) |
   378	| (a″) root twist λm | ✗ (DI) | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | yes |
   379	| (b) kN, N·w | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ (nothing to spread; w > B kills smoothness) | ✗ | ✗ | yes |
   380	| (c) f(x + t), u^d f(x/u), Möbius | ✗ (DI) | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | yes (heights grow like t^d, u^d) |
   381	| (d) the O(D²) special fields | ✗ (RB) | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | yes |
   382	| (e) random special-q only | ✗ (RB: union over ℓ ⊂ values on a disc) | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | yes |
   383	| (f) f₀ + (x − m)c, fixed g | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ (Res fixed = r^jN) | ✗ | ✗ | **no** (PH: height ≥ (m−1)‖c‖) |
   384	| (f′) f₀ + G·c with random g, w | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | **no**: this is Theorem U's design; f̂_{g,w} no longer sees f₀ |
   385	
   386	So, **proved**: every device that keeps ‖f‖ = N^{o(1/d)} keeps the field and the value set (HR, RS, DI). Its union of
   387	values is N^{2ν+o(1)}-sparse, and relativising supply proofs fail (RB). Every device that restores a full-support field
   388	law pays height ≥ N^{1/d} (HR, PH) and lands in Theorem U's design, which is GNFS-like.
   389	
