   391	
   392	## 3. Special families with natural rigour
   393	
   394	### 3.1 Prop CY (cyclotomic pieces) [proved, conditional on Theorem U]
   395	
   396	- Write r^e − 1 = ∏_{a|e}Φ_a(r) and r^e + 1 = ∏_{a|2e, a∤e}Φ_a(r).
   397	- Each piece satisfies Φ_a(r) ≤ (r + 1)^{φ(a)}, since |r − ζ| ≤ r + 1, and φ(a) ≤ φ(e) for a | e.
   398	- So every piece has log-size ≤ ε_e·log N·(1 + O(1/log N)), with ε_e := φ(e)log(r + 1)/(e log r). For r^e + 1 use
   399	  φ(2e) instead of φ(e).
   400	
   401	**Prop CY.** Completely factor each piece with Theorem U's algorithm, after computing the pieces (polynomially many
   402	integer operations, `cyclo.algebraic_pieces`, which never factors N). This factors N = r^e ± 1 in expected time
   403	L_N[2/5, c_U·ε_e^{2/5} + o(1)].
   404	
   405	*Proof.*
   406	- There are τ(e) ≤ e = O(log N) pieces.
   407	- Theorem U gives, for every η > 0, a constant C_η with T_U(n) ≤ C_η + L_n[2/5, c_U + η].
   408	- L_n[2/5, c] is increasing in n, and log Φ_a(r) ≤ ε_e t. Hence L_{Φ_a(r)}[2/5, c] ≤ exp(c(ε_et)^{2/5}(log t)^{3/5}) =
   409	  L_N[2/5, cε_e^{2/5}].
   410	- Pieces that share primes (those dividing e) only need a final merge.
   411	- Correctness follows from Theorem U and AKS certification. ∎
   412	
   413	**Corollary CY-P.** Along e = p_k# (primorials), ε_e ≤ (1 + o(1))log(r + 1)/(log r)·∏_{p≤p_k}(1 − 1/p) → 0 (Mertens),
   414	so N_e = r^e − 1 factors in L_{N_e}[2/5, o(1)]. This is an infinite natural family with a strictly better rigorous
   415	*constant*, but the same α. `cyclo.largest_piece_log_ratio` gives 0.31, 0.234 and 0.208 for e = 30, 210 and 2310
   416	(r = 2).
   417	
   418	**Aurifeuillian factors.** Where they exist (e.g. 2^{4k+2} + 1 = (2^{2k+1} − 2^{k+1} + 1)(2^{2k+1} + 2^{k+1} + 1);
   419	in general Brent 1993 and Stevenhagen 1987), they split Φ_{2e}(r) into two halves of equal size. That multiplies the
   420	constant by 2^{−2/5} for those (r, e) (`cyclo.special_pieces`, r = 2). Since φ(e) ≥ e/(e^γ log log e + 3/log log e),
   421	the primitive piece always has log-size ≥ t/O(log log t). So **no exponent gain is possible from algebraic factors**:
   422	the primitive part is a general-looking integer of size N^{Ω(1/log log log N)}.
   423	
   424	### 3.2 Families defined by their factors (codex snfsrig_1 §4) [proved; recorded for completeness]
   425	
   426	These are trivially fast, but they are not special *forms*.
   427	- Semiprimes with q − p ≤ (log p)²: one Fermat step, polynomial time. There are infinitely many by the prime number
   428	  theorem.
   429	- Squarefree N with every p − 1 being L[β]-smooth: Pollard p − 1 with random bases gives expected L[β]. A Cunningham
   430	  representation implies nothing of the kind. (For p | Φ_e(r) one only knows e | p − 1, which is log e bits.)
   431	
   432	---
   433	
   434	## 4. The precise obstruction (what would suffice)
   435	
   436	Take the Eisenstein family 𝔈. The size model of §1.4 identifies two sufficient inputs for a rigorous L[1/3] in the
   437	Dixon-split + rational-only-descent architecture.
   438	
   439	**(O1) Hypothesis RT (rational-target supply in the Kummer fields) [open].** There are constants (b, b₁, s, λ, φ_F)
   440	such that, for all large N = r^e − s ∈ 𝔈 and every prime q ∈ (e^{bE}, e^{b₁Q}] not dividing rsN, the following holds.
   441	With d = d(log q) as in §1.4, the reduced-basis box of radius e^{sE} of {(a, b) : a ≡ bm_d (mod q)} contains a primitive
   442	(a, b) such that
   443	- F_d(a, b) = a^d − sr^{j_d}b^d is e^{bE}-smooth, and
   444	- (a − bm_d)/q is q^λ-smooth (large primes coprime to rsN).
   445	
   446	This is the rational-target-only, special-field case of [nest] Hypothesis AD. AD itself is not needed here (no
   447	algebraic targets). In the random-integer model RT holds with room (§1.4: good-point margin 2s − (φ_F + φ_R)/3 > 0,
   448	union bound over e^{O(Q)} targets).
   449	
   450	**(O2) ECM-speed recognition [status: see §5].** A rigorous procedure that, for all p in some set G of primes of
   451	density ≥ (log x)^{−C} in every dyadic range (x, 2x] with x ≤ L_N[2/3], finds every factor p ∈ G of its input in
   452	expected time L_p[1/2, O(1)].
   453	
   454	**Statement [model, not proved].** RT + (O2) + LV6 at degree O(D′) in each Kummer field, with multi-field row algebra
   455	(one algebraic block per K_d, [nest] Prop R), give a Las Vegas L[1/3, c] algorithm for 𝔈. I have not written this
   456	implication out. It is a sufficiency claim at the level of [nest] Theorem NR's proof. Theorem NR's single-field
   457	argument and transcript invariance carry over; the multi-field LV6 square roots and character columns are UNVERIFIED
   458	bookkeeping. Note that [nest] Theorem NR cannot simply be specialised to a point-mass field law: its recursive calls
   459	need the algorithm for *all* integers. That is (O2) again.
   460	
   461	**How far (O1) is from known results.**
   462	- *Algebraic half.* RT needs F_d(a, b) = a^d − sr^jb^d, of degree d growing up to D′, to be y-smooth with
   463	  log y ≍ (log|F|)^{1/2+o(1)}: u → ∞ like (log X)^{1/2}. And it needs this *pointwise* in each special-q box.
   464	  BBDT 2012 give positive proportions for fixed degree-d binary forms only at y = x^{d−2+ε} (x^{1/√e+ε} for cubics),
   465	  where x is the variable size: power-size y, bounded u. Barbulescu–Lachand 2017, Thm 4.2, reach
   466	  y ≥ exp((log log X)^{5/3+ε}), but for fixed imaginary *quadratic* forms of fundamental discriminant, unrestricted
   467	  boxes, and without joint smoothness with a rational form (codex). Nothing is known at growing degree or in special-q
   468	  lattices.
   469	- *Rational half.* The values a − b·r^{q_d} with |a|, |b| ≤ V are exactly the integers ≤ r^{q_d}V whose base-r
   470	  digits in positions [log_r V, q_d) are all 0 or all r − 1 (those of a mod r^{q_d}). That is a proportion
   471	  (1 − dν)/(1 + dν) → 1 of their digits. Prescribed-digit theorems for primes (Bourgain 2013/2015, Swaenepoel 2020)
   472	  allow only a small positive proportion of prescribed digits. Even the rational half of RT, for smooth numbers instead
   473	  of primes, is far beyond them. Also, by Prop RB, statistics of smooth numbers cannot replace it.
   474	
