## Verdict

**Yes, conditionally on the project's internal chain: a rigorous Las Vegas L_N[2/5, c] factoring algorithm for every N.**

- **Theorem U [proved, conditional on the internal unrefereed chain; see §10 for the exact dependency list].**
  Fix rationals a, κ, d₀ > 0. The algorithm FACTOR_U[a, κ, d₀] of §1 is Las Vegas and outputs the complete
  prime factorisation of every integer N ≥ 2. Its expected running time is
  L_N[2/5, c + o(1)], where c = c(a, κ, d₀) < ∞ is given by (10.3) in terms of one exponent C_γ = C_γ(a, κ, d₀)
  (the polylogarithmic precision loss of the relation supply, §7).
  - Parameters: d ≍ D^{1/5} (odd), k ≍ D^{2/5}, B = exp(aΛ), where D = log N/log log N and
    Λ = (log N)^{2/5}(log log N)^{3/5}. Special-q primes are L[3/5], norms are N^{θ}, θ ≍ D^{−1/5}.
  - For illustration (exact rational grid search, `params.optimise`): c ≤ 3.52, 4.05, 5.04 and 6.50 for
    C_γ = 0, 1, 5 and 20 (grid upper bounds; a finer grid gives 3.51, 4.04, 5.04, 6.47). We do not compute C_γ numerically: it involves the unspecified absolute constants of
    Hildebrand–Tenenbaum's saddle-point estimates.
  - Hence also L[α, o(1)] for every α > 2/5, and in particular L[3/7].
- **The precision exponent is τ = 0 for every ingredient** (table below). This is the decisive question of
  recurse §3.5, and the answer is that the feared d^{O(d)}, c^d and (log N)^{O(d)} losses are all artifacts:
  - of two wide windows (b ∈ (V/2, V] and the dyadic special-q range, each raised to the power ≈ 2d in LD*);
  - of an exponential constant 2^{2d+5} kept in the norm envelope X_F although it is multiplied by N^{−η₀/2};
  - of a cube-section volume bound in Lemma Kern, which costs ((d+1)H)^{d−2}/H^{d−2}.
  The repairs are a b-window of relative width 1/d, one common box radius V for all special-q primes, the
  envelope X_F := (2d+1)MHV^d, and a dimension-free triangular fibre count (Lemma Kern-U, §3). They cost only
  polynomial factors in d, which are (log N)^{O(1)}.
  - The size model of recurse §3.3 then gives α = (2 + τ)/(5 + 2τ) = **2/5**, attained (not just approached),
    with a finite constant.
- **GPT-6.1-sol, in a blind round (`codex/lv5unif_1`, same base texts, before my draft), reached the same verdict**: "the apparent τ = 1
  barrier is removable … the repaired one-level framework reaches 2/5; 3/7 is not forced by precision". It found
  the same three repairs (with a narrow ℓ-window instead of a common V). It also supplied a counterexample showing
  that Lemma Kern at the bare height H = V² really has C_d ≥ (3/2)^d/9, so the height margin H ≥ dV² is needed,
  and a failure mode of Lemma W when η₀ shrinks too fast (η₀ = (log N)^{−2}). Both are respected here.
- **Below 2/5: not reached.** The floor is the one-level descent geometry (split acceptance (log N)^{−2k} forces
  k = O(D^α); the norm exponent θ ≥ c/√k forces k ≳ D^{2(1−2α)}), not precision (§13). Multi-level descent is
  the open problem, as recurse §3.4 says.
- **The shared-rational-side multi-field trick works (Proposition MF, §11) [moment part proved; multi-field
  post-processing proved in outline].** Conditional on the rational data, the fields are independent, and a
  pointwise first-moment bound at one good rational candidate suffices. So the algebraic side needs no second moment
  at all and only exp(o(Λ))·ρ_F precision, at the price of ≈ 1/ρ_F = L[2/5]^{O(1)} fields and one extra height floor
  log H ≥ 13(σ + 1)dΛ log log N (needed so that rejected fields cannot absorb the pointwise mass; codex lv5unif_2 #3).
  It would give a second route to L[2/5] that avoids LD*, Lemma Kern, L2_fam and Lemma W, once the multi-field
  post-processing is written out in full; it still uses Lemma GM-U, Theorem R, LV6 and the internal chain, so it is not
  independent of them. It does not go below 2/5, because it does not change the geometry.
- **LV6 at growing degree [proved, §9].** Stark's factorial is harmless: character primes of
  s = ⌈(2d)!·(log N + log Δ̄ + d)⁴⌉ bits satisfy every requirement of Lemmas E and X, and cost exp(O(d log d)) =
  L[2/5]^{o(1)}, because d log d ≍ D^{1/5}log log N = o(Λ).
- **η₀ bookkeeping [proved, §12].** η₀ := 1/⌈D^{1/2}⌉. Then every N^{η₀}-margin is L_N[1/2, 1/3]-large, which beats
  every L[2/5]^{O(1)}; Lemma W's constant is 24/η₀ = O((log N)^{1/2}); and η₀ = o(θ), so the slack does not move c.
- **Literature [§14].** We found no rigorous L[α, c] with α < 1/2 for factoring arbitrary N, unconditional or under
  GRH. The best rigorous bound for arbitrary N that we found is Lenstra–Pomerance 1992, L[1/2, 1 + o(1)].
  Lee–Venkatesan 2018 prove unconditionally, for every n, that a randomised NFS finds a possibly trivial congruence of
  squares in L[1/3, (64/9)^{1/3} + o(1)] (their Thm 2.1); their non-triviality theorem (Thm 2.3) covers only semiprimes
  with p ≡ q ≡ 3 (mod 4) and assumes their Conjecture 7.1. We found no prior formulation, heuristic or rigorous, of an
  L[2/5] Dixon-split algorithm with one-level NFS-type descent. This is a search result, not a novelty claim.

**The weakest points** (my assessment, shared by codex):
1. Everything rests on the internal fixed-degree chain (hypD, lemmaM, pcest, lv6, ldbound, lem85, recurse), which
   no outside referee has read. Theorem U does not add a new kind of input; it re-proves that chain with explicit
   constants and three design changes.
2. The one external analytic input of substance is still Drappeau's Lemme 5 (Compositio 2015), whose published
   proof refers to §3.3 of Harper's unpublished preprint 1208.5992 ([lem85] §2).
3. Bookkeeping: LD*'s matching of geometric envelopes against smoothness densities is where an error would most
   likely hide. §6 does this matching explicitly.

### τ table (precision exponent at growing (d, k); loss log(1/γ) ≍ d^τ log log N)

| ingredient | where d, k enter as written in the fixed-degree sources | exponential? | repair | τ after repair |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| FM (first moment) | #𝒫 ≍ V²/(d log V); one-point smoothing: TV ≤ 3d²V/H, \|M₀\| ≤ dV^{d−1}; ϱ-argument shifts 2^{O(d)}ϖ² (cost exp(O(d log log N/Λ)) = 1 + o(1)); C_d = 2^{2d+5} only against N^{−η₀/2} | no | b-window and common V for matching with LD*; X_F = (2d+1)MHV^d | **0** |
| Lemma 8.5′ (restricted BV) | none, once restated in a regime (R′) that does not mention L[1/2] | no | regime (R′), §5 | **0** |
| Lemma Kern | cube-section volume ((d+1)H)^{d−2}; (V²/bb′)^{d−2} ≤ 4^{d−2}; codex: C_d ≥ (3/2)^d/9 at H = V² | **yes**, d^{O(d)} | triangular fibre count + H ≥ dV² + b-window: Pr ≤ e/((2H+1)²(bb′)^{d−2}) | **0** |
| L2_fam | (log N)^{O(C₀)} with C₀ ≍ log(1/(ρ_rρ_F))/log B; the old cushion L_N[1/2,1] would force C₀ ≳ D^{1/10} | only via the cushion | cushion B instead of L[1/2] (codex) | **0** |
| LD* (second moment) | Kern's C_d; (V_max/V₋)^{2d} ≤ 2^d; (2^{2d+5})² in Ψ(X_F)²; "≤ 12 partners" (absolute); C_W | **yes**, 2^{O(d)} | common V; b-window; X_F = (2d+1)MHV^d; Kern-U | **0** |
| Lemma W | C_W = 24/η₀ | no, if η₀ log N ≫ Λ + log S | η₀ = D^{−1/2} | **0** |
| Theorem K / S″ | the design's split cost p ≥ (γ/4)(γ/(16 log N))^{2k}/(32k²); no C(2k, k), no 2^ω | k enters only as the (by design) L[2/5]-size split cost | Lemma S″ replaces S′ | **0** |
| Lemma CS (computable S) | the old explicit S lost de Bruijn's second-order term (thmA U5), and S^d must be inside X_F | — | Rankin value at an approximate saddle point, evaluated at the actual X_F(S) | **0** |
| LV6 | Stark ν₁ ≍ 1/((2d)! log Δ̄); SQ's 2^{d−1} sign vectors; t_χ = poly(d, log N) | cost only, exp(O(d log d)) = L[2/5]^{o(1)} | factorial-bit character primes | not a γ-loss |
| Lemma IR, rejections | exp(−log H/(13 d log log H)) = exp(−(log N)^{2/5−o(1)}) | no | — | not a γ-loss |

So α = (2 + 0)/(5 + 0) = 2/5.

---

## 1. Parameters and the algorithm FACTOR_U

**Notation.** For the integer N handed to SPLIT (after preprocessing): λ := log log N, D := log N/λ,
Λ := D^{2/5}λ = (log N)^{2/5}(log log N)^{3/5}, and L_N[2/5, c] = e^{cΛ}. "L^{o(1)}" now means exp(o(Λ)),
uniformly in N. All "C" below depend only on (a, κ, d₀) and on the absolute constants of the cited theorems.
**They never depend on d, k or N.**

**Fixed parameters.** Rationals a, κ, d₀ > 0.

**N-dependent parameters** (all computable in time (log N)^{O(1)}, except S, which costs B(log N)^{O(1)}; §2):
- d := the least odd integer ≥ max(3, d₀D^{1/5}); k := max(⌈κD^{2/5}⌉, d + 1); ν := 1/(4k).
- η₀ := 1/⌈D^{1/2}⌉.
- μ := μ_opt(d, k) of [pp] Lemma `lem:optmu` (exact rational); h := h_*(μ) + η₀ with
  h_*(μ) = max(2ν, μ − (d−2)ν, 1 − dμ + ν); θ := 2μ + h + (d−1)ν.
  Then θ·D^{1/5} → θ₀(d₀, κ) := 3/d₀ if κ ≥ d₀²/4, and 2/d₀ + d₀/(4κ) otherwise (`params.theta0`). (At fixed
  d the k-term of the level regime is lower order: 4ν = 1/k ≍ D^{−2/5}.)
- B := ⌊e^{aΛ}⌋. X := ⌊√(N/2)⌋; j maximal with 2^{kj} ≤ X; Y := 2^{j−1}; J := primes in (Y, 2Y]. So
  log Y = log N/(2k) + O(1) = Λ_{3/5}/(2κ)(1 + o(1)), i.e. ℓ = L_N[3/5, 1/(2κ)].
- M := ⌊N^μ⌋, H := ⌊N^h⌋, W := ⌈N^{μ+ν+η₀/2}⌉ (integer roots of N^p for the rational exponents).
- S: the first S = 2^j in the scan j = 0, 1, 2, … with S²·R(X_r(S))·R(X_F(S)) ≥ 1 (Lemma CS), where
  **V(S) := ⌊√(2Y)·S⌋, common to all ℓ ∈ J**, X_r(S) := 2MV/Y and **X_F(S) := (2d + 1)·MHV^d**.
  Put V := V(S), X_r, X_F at this S, ρ_r := ϱ(X_r), ρ_F := ϱ(X_F) (ϱ(z) = Ψ(z, B)/z).
- Candidates (one set for all ℓ): **𝒫 := {(a, b) ∈ Z² : b prime, (1 − 1/d)V < b ≤ V, |a| ≤ V, gcd(a, b) = 1}.**
- Filter: |G(P)| ≥ MV/32. Counted points, N_ℓ(A), descendable set 𝒟_A, δ_A and γ as in [pp] §3.3, with 𝒫 and V.

The analysis-only parameters (never used by the algorithm): ϖ := B²S² (truncation, FM), the grid
𝒳 := {X_F ϖ^{−2}(3/2)^i} ∩ [X_Fϖ^{−2}, 16dX_F], and R₀ := B^{C₀} with C₀ the least integer such that
B^{C₀} ≥ 64S²B/(ρ_rρ_F) (L2_fam; note the cushion B, not L_N[1/2, 1], following codex lv5unif_1 §1(d)).

**The algorithm FACTOR_U[a, κ, d₀]** is [pp] Algorithm `alg:factor` (FACTOR, with SPLIT and trial division run in
alternation, so there is no N₀ branch) with SPLIT, SAMPLE_FIELD, DESCEND_ONCE, SQ_SEARCH and POSTPROCESS of [pp]
§3.4 and §8, changed as follows:
1. the parameters above, with the common V and the b-window in 𝒫;
2. DESCEND_ONCE's shape test calls **FACTOR_U itself** on |u₀| and |v₀| with fresh coins (recursion, [rec] §1.1);
3. POSTPROCESS uses character primes of s := ⌈(2d)!·(log N + log Δ̄ + d)⁴⌉ bits (§9);
4. the row target M_rows := C_col + 12 as in [pp] (eq:Mrows), with t_χ of §9.

The shape test accepts iff |u₀| and |v₀| are each products of at most k distinct primes, all in J, as in [pp].

*Common V in the special-q search.* In SQ_SEARCH and in [pp] Lemma `lem:reg`, V_ℓ is replaced by V and S by
S_ℓ := V/√ℓ ∈ [S(1 − o(1)), √2·S]. Lemma `lem:reg` then reads: if λ₁ < √ℓ/(2S_ℓ) = ℓ/(2V), the lattice has no
candidate (the line spacing ℓ/|w₀| exceeds 2V ≥ √2V, and |w₀| < ℓ/(2V) < (1 − 1/d)V < b because
ℓ ≤ 2Y < 2(1 − 1/d)V², which holds for every S ≥ 1 since V² ≥ YS²(1 − o(1)) and d ≥ 3; auditU #4);
- The `rec`/`pb` time ratio is 1.1–37. Pollard–Brent is far cheaper than any L-type algorithm at toy sizes, so the
  ratio has no asymptotic meaning.
- **The z ≈ −2.2 in P5.** It is inside the frozen threshold, but it is the largest deviation seen so far in the
  project's DD checks (thmA E1: −0.82, +0.29). Nearly all of the tested dependencies come from the tiny inner moduli.
  The 8 dependencies per LV6 round are linearly independent, so they are independent fair tests under DD. We see no
  mechanism for a bias and record it as a fluctuation (two-sided p ≈ 0.026).

E1 checks the code and the exact Lemma OS. It cannot test any asymptotic claim.

## 3. Growing degree

Notation: Λ_α := (log N)^α(log log N)^{1−α}, so L_N[α, c] = exp(cΛ_α). D := log N/log log N.

### 3.1 Recursion at L[α]: the shape-test barrier disappears [proved, conditional on the outer algorithm]

Suppose some design has its non-shape work bounded by L_N[α, p + o(1)] and makes L_N[α, δ + o(1)] expected shape
tests on inputs ≤ √(2N), with all the structure of §1.2. Then Lemma F holds with Λ_α in place of Λ.
- The scaling step becomes L_{√(2N)}[α, b] ≤ e·L_N[α, 2^{−α}b].
- The conclusion is c = max(p, δ/(1 − 2^{−α})).

So the cost of complete factorisation of the halves never forces α. This is lemmaM's barrier (c): HECM at
B1 = L[2/3] costs L[4/9]. The recursive replacement costs L_{√N}[α] = L_N[α, 2^{−α}c].

Every intermediate smoothness test, on a value x = exp((log N)^{γ}) with γ < 1, costs
L_x[α, c] = exp(c(log N)^{γα}…) = L_N[α]^{o(1)}. So multi-level designs need neither HECM nor batch smoothness at the
large intermediate bounds.

**Caveat.** This replaces every cost barrier of testing. It does nothing for relation supply or descent geometry.

### 3.2 Lemma S″: split counting without the 2^ω loss [proved]

dependency S the accumulated ∏_{i∈S}B_i is used as an explicit square correction when forming Y (replacing Z_i by
A_iB_i *without* that correction would change the residue row). Characters are unaffected because character primes
divide no atom (Lemma X_ERH).

**Lemma TS (tree size) [proved].** In any field, a DESC tree has depth ≤ ⌈log(b₁Q/(bE))/log(1/λ)⌉ + 1 = O(log t)
(children of a target e^z are ≤ e^{λz}, and targets are > B) and each node has ≤ 2·(log|F(P)| + log|G(P)|)/log B
= O(t/E) children, so ≤ e^{O((log t)²)} = e^{o(E)} nodes. The box has ≤ (2e^{sE} + 1)² points. (codex (30).)

**Lemma T_multi (transcript invariance for adaptive multi-level trees) [proved].** Every exposed quantity of NEST
(field, R_i, (u₀, v₀), canonical factorisations returned by recursive calls, all box scans, all failed and
successful DESC calls and their order, field restarts, columns, characters, kernel vectors, Hensel prime, Y) is a
function of (R_i) and coins independent of the hidden roots: DESC is a deterministic function of (A, target) given
the canonical factorisations, and recursive calls use fresh coins (recurse Lemmas OS, T_rec). Hence, as in Theorem
DD, X/Y is uniform on μ₂(Z/N) given the transcript, and Pr[1 < gcd(X − Y, N) < N | 𝒯] = 1 − 2^{1−ω(N)} ≥ 1/2.
(codex (34)–(35) gives the same argument; the dependency must be chosen before the hidden roots are used, and after a
trivial gcd fresh rows are drawn, as in lv6 §3.7.) ∎

*Proof of Theorem NR.*
- **Correctness.** Every output is certified (AKS, gcds), so the algorithm is Las Vegas; correctness never uses AD.
- **Field restarts.** By AD a field is good with probability ≥ 1/2; in a good field DESC never returns FAIL (every
  target has a good point, and its children are again targets). A bad field either fails (restart) or produces valid
  rows. By Lemma TS the work of every row attempt is bounded by the same quantity in good and bad fields, so the
  expected number of fields is ≤ 2 and the expected cost is at most twice the good-field bound.
- **Split acceptance.** Let k := ⌈t/(2b₁Q)⌉ + 1, X := ⌊√(N/2)⌋/2, Y := ⌊X^{1/k}/2⌋ (so B < Y < 2Y ≤ e^{b₁Q}), and
  𝒫 := primes in (Y, 2Y] not dividing D_A (all but O(t/Q) of them). After trial division to B, 2^{ω(N)} ≤ e^{O(t/E)}
  while |𝒫| ≥ Y/(3 log Y) = e^{(1+o(1))Q·(b₁ − o(1))} ≥ 4k³2^ω. Lemma S″ (recurse §3.2; codex (26)) with 𝒰 := all
  products of k distinct primes of 𝒫 (≤ (2Y)^k ≤ X, 2X² < N) gives Pr[accept] ≥ |𝒰|²/(2φ(N)) ≥ (|𝒫|/k)^{2k}/(2N)
  ≥ exp(−2k log(6k log Y) − O(k)) = e^{−(1/b₁ + o(1))E}, using 2k log(6k log Y) = (D′/b₁)(log t)(1 + o(1)).
  (Lemma S″ is used without the lower end of its window, a harmless generalisation; codex nested_2 issue 11.)
  Each attempt costs two recursive calls on integers ≤ √(2N).
- **Descent.** Per accepted row: e^{o(E)} nodes (Lemma TS), each scanning ≤ (2e^{sE} + 1)² points, each point
  costing two recursive factorisations of integers ≤ max(|F(P)|, |G(P)|) ≤ N^{τ_F + o(1)}, since with
  ‖P‖_∞ ≤ 3e^{sE}q: log|F(P)| ≤ log(d + 1) + κQ + d(z + sE + log 3) ≤ δD′(b₁Q + sE) + O(Q) = τ_F·t + O(Q) and
  log|G(P)| ≤ κQ + z + sE + O(1) = O(Q).
- **Linear algebra.** #columns ≤ (d + 2)π(B) + O(d²t) = e^{(b+o(1))E}; row weight e^{o(E)}; Wiedemann/KS
  e^{(2b+o(1))E}; Lemma SQ's Hensel lifting to e^{(b+o(1))E} digits (exponents ≤ e^{O(log² t)}): e^{(b+o(1))E}·poly.
  Expected number of LV6 rounds ≤ 2 (Lemma T_multi).
- **Recursion.** With F(n) the worst expected cost of complete factorisation of integers ≤ n, the above gives
  F(N) ≤ e^{(2b+o(1))E} + e^{(b+1/b₁+o(1))E}F(√(2N)) + e^{(b+2s+o(1))E}F(N^{τ_F+o(1)}).
  Since Λ_{1/3}(n^τ) = (τ^{1/3} + o(1))Λ_{1/3}(n), strong induction (recurse §3.1; codex (32)) gives
  F(N) ≤ L_N[1/3, c] for every c > c_NR. ∎

**Remarks on Theorem NR.**
- **What AD replaces.** FM, LD*, Lemma 8.5′, Kern, Prop PZ and Theorem K (every target is good, so no density γ is
  needed and the 2^ω loss is absent by Lemma S″). The precision problem of recurse §3.3 disappears. ERH is used only in
  Lemma X_ERH (and could be replaced by LV §6).
- **AD is the standard heuristic (reduced-basis form).** Under the usual "values behave like random integers of
  their size" model, using the worst-case sizes log|F(P)| ≤ κ_FQ + d(z + sE) and log|G(P)/q|, log|G(P)| ≤ m₀Q + sE + O(z),
  every target (regular or not) has ≈ e^{(2s − φ/3 + o(1))E} good points with
  φ = (κ_F + m₀)/(λb) + δ/λ + δs/(λb) (the δ/λ term is δ/(2λ) for regular lattices, recurse §3.4 / codex (29)),
  so the failure probability exp(−e^{Ω(E)}) per target beats the union bound over e^{O(Q)} targets whenever
| multi-level (α < 2/5) | nested descendability (endogenous children) | **no tool** | no |

**Conclusion.**
- Recursion plus one-level descent would give a rigorous L[2/5, c] *if* the four "open" rows were proved with
  polylogarithmic precision uniform in (d, k), together with the LV6 and η₀ repairs below. These are sufficient
  inputs, not necessary conditions; the first draft's "iff" was unsupported (codex recurse_2). We expect C_d growth to make 3/7 the realistic first target.
- The decisive missing input is a quantitative, explicit-error version of LV §5, plus Kern with dimension-free
  constants, at special-q lattices. LV's L^{o(1)}-precision first moment is not enough, because γ is raised to the
  power 2k with k ≍ D^α.
- One possible route around the precision requirement is UNVERIFIED. It uses several fields that share the rational
  side G = g₁x − g₀y (same g, independent (w_j, c_j), Coppersmith-style). Then only the rational-side statement (PC_rat,
  Theorem R) needs polylog precision, and the algebraic side needs only L^{−o(1)}. This needs conditional-on-g moments
  that nobody has proved.

## 4. Prior art (Opus literature subagent, primary sources where accessible; 2026-10-20)

- **Lenstra–Pomerance, JAMS 5 (1992)** [read]. Algorithm 10.1 factors the form coefficient a ≈ √n with the ECM-based
  Algorithm 7.2, at L^{o(1)} per number (Thm 7.3). The only recursion is on factors already found: Algorithm 7.2, and
  Algorithm 10.4 / Thm 10.5 on at most log n divisors. The authors note the time is exactly L[1/2, 1 + o(1)] and that
  "one cannot improve this by choosing the parameter y differently" (p. 509).
- **Pomerance 1987** (Discrete Algorithms and Complexity) [read].
  - Thm 2.1: an ECM smoothness procedure that recurses on found factors.
  - pp. 138–139: inside the discrete-log algorithm, one option is "to apply algorithm REC … to p − 1, completely
    factoring it". This is the closest precedent for using one's own rigorous factoring algorithm as a subroutine, but
    it is a single call on a different number, not a recursion that feeds a fixed point.
- Seysen 1987: not accessible. LP cite L[1/2, √(5/4)] under GRH. Lenstra 1987 (2.12) says Seysen's smoothness test is
  Pollard ρ / Pollard–Strassen.
- Lenstra–Pila–Pomerance I/II: HECM, with no recursion. Part III, which LPP cite for details, was apparently never
  published.
- Schnorr–Lenstra 1984: "recursion" there is a group walk. Dixon 1981 and Pomerance 1982: early-abort induction on the
  number of aborts, not on N.
- Vallée 1991: abstract only.
- Lee–Venkatesan 2018 [read]: HECM (Fact 3.18, Cor. 3.19) for L[2/3] norms. No recursion.

