   342	Hence a proof of γ_𝔇 > 0 that uses only inputs of type (ii), and inputs of type (iii) whose error budgets exceed
   343	|𝒱|·|ℐ|, would also prove γ_𝔇(𝒮′) > 0, contradicting (i). Such a proof therefore cannot exist. In the SNFS regime the
   344	budgets do exceed |𝒱|·|ℐ| (next paragraph).
   345	
   346	*Proof.* (i): every success requires some G_i(P)/ℓ ∈ 𝒮, and 𝒱 ∩ 𝒮′ = ∅. (ii): monotonicity. (iii): for each I,
   347	|#(𝒮_B ∩ I) − #(𝒮′ ∩ I)| ≤ |𝒱|. ∎
   348	
   349	**The SNFS numbers [proved arithmetic].**
   350	- *Size of 𝒱.* Φ = (log N)^{O(1)} (the fields K_d, d ≤ D, together with devices whose T have entries
   351	  (log N)^{O(1)}). Also π_J = N^{2ν+o(1)} and W = L^{O(1)}, so |𝒱| = N^{2ν+o(1)}.
   352	- *Size of the counted values.* The rational values have size X_r = N^{1/d−ν+o(1)}.
   353	- *Inputs on intervals or single APs.* These count sets I of integers of size x ≥ X_r with expected size
   354	  ≥ xρ/B^{O(1)} and budget ε·(main), ε = L^{−O(1)}. Their deletion error |𝒱| is negligible because 1/d − ν > 2ν.
   355	- *Barban–Davenport–Halberstam / Bombieri–Vinogradov-type sums* (Lemma 8.5′). These range over |ℐ| ≤ x^{1/2} classes,
   356	  with budget ≍ ε·Ψ(x). The deletion error is ≤ |𝒱|·x^{1/2}, which is ≤ ε·xρ iff |𝒱| ≤ ερx^{1/2}. At x ≥ X_r this holds
   357	  iff 2ν < (1/d − ν)/2, i.e. **5ν < 1/d (d < 4k/5)**.
   358	- *Regime.* This holds throughout the regime that matters: at the 2/5 design d ≍ D^{1/5} and ν ≍ D^{−2/5}, and for every
   359	  fixed d once k > 5d/4. Algebraic values are larger still.
   360	
   361	*Contrast with Theorem U [consistency check].* There the union over the field law of the values F_A(P) is an AP of
   362	length ≍ HV^{d−1}. The point masses are ≤ 1/((2H + 1)V′^{d−1}) ([U] Lemma SM1). Deleting K values changes FM by
   363	≤ K·#𝒫·max mass, which is negligible for any K = N^{O(ν)}. So the same statistical inputs do suffice. The barrier is
   364	the small support of a fixed family, exactly Prop SI(i)/(ii) of [nest] but for the plain smooth set.
   365	
   366	**What a proof would need instead.** It would need non-relativising, form-specific information: arithmetic of the
   367	specific values (Norm(a − bα₀) in a Kummer field, a − b·r^j in base r), such as type I/II sums for incomplete norm forms
   368	(Heath-Brown, Maynard), BBDT's methods for binary forms, or prescribed-digit methods (Bourgain). §4–§5 compare the
   369	required ranges with what these give.
   370	
   371	### 2.6 Device table (what survives)
   372	
   373	"✗" = the ingredient's hypotheses fail. "✓" = it holds, but only by giving up the SNFS norms.
   374	
   375	| device | (R) Theorem R | (GM) | (K) Kern | (W) | FM | LD* | keeps SNFS norms? |
   376	|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   377	| (a) random rational side, special root | ✗ (RS: only ±(x − m) below N^{1−1/d}) | — | — | — | ✗ | ✗ | yes, but then fixed g |
   378	| (a′) random root m′ (BLP) | ✓ | needs a new f | — | — | GNFS | GNFS | **no** (base-m′ heights) |
   379	| (a″) root twist λm | ✗ (DI) | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | yes |
   380	| (b) kN, N·w | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ (nothing to spread; w > B kills smoothness) | ✗ | ✗ | yes |
   381	| (c) f(x + t), u^d f(x/u), Möbius | ✗ (DI) | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | yes (heights grow like t^d, u^d) |
   382	| (d) the O(D²) special fields | ✗ (RB) | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | yes |
   383	| (e) random special-q only | ✗ (RB: union over ℓ ⊂ values on a disc) | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | ✗ | yes |
   384	| (f) f₀ + (x − m)c, fixed g | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | ✗ (Res fixed = r^jN) | ✗ | ✗ | **no** (PH: height ≥ (m−1)‖c‖) |
   385	| (f′) f₀ + G·c with random g, w | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ | **no**: this is Theorem U's design; f̂_{g,w} no longer sees f₀ |
   386	
   387	So, **proved**: every device that keeps ‖f‖ = N^{o(1/d)} keeps the field and the value set (HR, RS, DI). Its union of
   388	values is N^{2ν+o(1)}-sparse, and relativising supply proofs fail (RB). Every device that restores a full-support field
   389	law pays height ≥ N^{1/d} (HR, PH) and lands in Theorem U's design, which is GNFS-like.
   390	
   400	  φ(2e) instead of φ(e).
   401	
   402	**Prop CY.** Completely factor each piece with Theorem U's algorithm, after computing the pieces (polynomially many
   403	integer operations, `cyclo.algebraic_pieces`, which never factors N). This factors N = r^e ± 1 in expected time
   404	L_N[2/5, c_U·ε_e^{2/5} + o(1)].
   405	
   406	*Proof.*
   407	- There are τ(e) ≤ e = O(log N) pieces.
   408	- Theorem U gives, for every η > 0, a constant C_η with T_U(n) ≤ C_η + L_n[2/5, c_U + η].
   409	- L_n[2/5, c] is increasing in n, and log Φ_a(r) ≤ ε_e t. Hence L_{Φ_a(r)}[2/5, c] ≤ exp(c(ε_et)^{2/5}(log t)^{3/5}) =
   410	  L_N[2/5, cε_e^{2/5}].
   411	- Pieces that share primes (those dividing e) only need a final merge.
   412	- Correctness follows from Theorem U and AKS certification. ∎
   413	
   414	**Corollary CY-P.** Along e = p_k# (primorials), ε_e ≤ (1 + o(1))log(r + 1)/(log r)·∏_{p≤p_k}(1 − 1/p) → 0 (Mertens),
   415	so N_e = r^e − 1 factors in L_{N_e}[2/5, o(1)]. This is an infinite natural family with a strictly better rigorous
   416	*constant*, but the same α. `cyclo.largest_piece_log_ratio` gives 0.31, 0.234 and 0.208 for e = 30, 210 and 2310
   417	(r = 2).
   418	
   419	**Aurifeuillian factors.** Where they exist (e.g. 2^{4k+2} + 1 = (2^{2k+1} − 2^{k+1} + 1)(2^{2k+1} + 2^{k+1} + 1);
   420	in general Brent 1993 and Stevenhagen 1987), they split Φ_{2e}(r) into two halves of equal size. That multiplies the
   421	constant by 2^{−2/5} for those (r, e) (`cyclo.special_pieces`, r = 2). Since φ(e) ≥ e/(e^γ log log e + 3/log log e),
   422	the primitive piece always has log-size ≥ t/O(log log t). So **no exponent gain is possible from algebraic factors**:
   423	the primitive part is a general-looking integer of size N^{Ω(1/log log log N)}.
   424	
   425	### 3.2 Families defined by their factors (codex snfsrig_1 §4) [proved; recorded for completeness]
   426	
   427	These are trivially fast, but they are not special *forms*.
   428	- Semiprimes with q − p ≤ (log p)²: one Fermat step, polynomial time. There are infinitely many by the prime number
   429	  theorem.
   430	- Squarefree N with every p − 1 being L[β]-smooth: Pollard p − 1 with random bases gives expected L[β]. A Cunningham
   431	  representation implies nothing of the kind. (For p | Φ_e(r) one only knows e | p − 1, which is log e bits.)
   432	
   433	---
   434	
   435	## 4. The precise obstruction (what would suffice)
   436	
   437	Take the Eisenstein family 𝔈. The size model of §1.4 identifies two sufficient inputs for a rigorous L[1/3] in the
   438	Dixon-split + rational-only-descent architecture.
   439	
   440	**(O1) Hypothesis RT (rational-target supply in the Kummer fields) [open].** There are constants (b, b₁, s, λ, φ_F)
   441	such that, for all large N = r^e − s ∈ 𝔈 and every prime q ∈ (e^{bE}, e^{b₁Q}] not dividing rsN, the following holds.
   442	With d = d(log q) as in §1.4, the reduced-basis box of radius e^{sE} of {(a, b) : a ≡ bm_d (mod q)} contains a primitive
   443	(a, b) such that
   444	- F_d(a, b) = a^d − sr^{j_d}b^d is e^{bE}-smooth, and
   445	- (a − bm_d)/q is q^λ-smooth (large primes coprime to rsN).
   446	
   447	This is the rational-target-only, special-field case of [nest] Hypothesis AD. AD itself is not needed here (no
   448	algebraic targets). In the random-integer model RT holds with room (§1.4: good-point margin 2s − (φ_F + φ_R)/3 > 0,
   449	union bound over e^{O(Q)} targets).
   450	
   451	**(O2) ECM-speed recognition [status: see §5].** A rigorous procedure that, for all p in some set G of primes of
   452	density ≥ (log x)^{−C} in every dyadic range (x, 2x] with x ≤ L_N[2/3], finds every factor p ∈ G of its input in
   453	expected time L_p[1/2, O(1)].
   454	
   455	**Statement [model, not proved].** RT + (O2) + LV6 at degree O(D′) in each Kummer field, with multi-field row algebra
   456	(one algebraic block per K_d, [nest] Prop R), give a Las Vegas L[1/3, c] algorithm for 𝔈. I have not written this
   457	implication out. It is a sufficiency claim at the level of [nest] Theorem NR's proof. Theorem NR's single-field
   458	argument and transcript invariance carry over; the multi-field LV6 square roots and character columns are UNVERIFIED
   459	bookkeeping. Note that [nest] Theorem NR cannot simply be specialised to a point-mass field law: its recursive calls
   460	need the algorithm for *all* integers. That is (O2) again.
   461	
   462	**How far (O1) is from known results.**
   463	- *Algebraic half.* RT needs F_d(a, b) = a^d − sr^jb^d, of degree d growing up to D′, to be y-smooth with
   464	  log y ≍ (log|F|)^{1/2+o(1)}: u → ∞ like (log X)^{1/2}. And it needs this *pointwise* in each special-q box.
   465	  BBDT 2012 give positive proportions for fixed degree-d binary forms only at y = x^{d−2+ε} (x^{1/√e+ε} for cubics),
   466	  where x is the variable size: power-size y, bounded u. Barbulescu–Lachand 2017, Thm 4.2, reach
   467	  y ≥ exp((log log X)^{5/3+ε}), but for fixed imaginary *quadratic* forms of fundamental discriminant, unrestricted
   468	  boxes, and without joint smoothness with a rational form (codex). Nothing is known at growing degree or in special-q
   469	  lattices.
   470	- *Rational half.* The values a − b·r^{q_d} with |a|, |b| ≤ V are exactly the integers ≤ r^{q_d}V whose base-r
   471	  digits in positions [log_r V, q_d) are all 0 or all r − 1 (those of a mod r^{q_d}). That is a proportion
   472	  (1 − dν)/(1 + dν) → 1 of their digits. Prescribed-digit theorems for primes (Bourgain 2013/2015, Swaenepoel 2020)
   473	  allow only a small positive proportion of prescribed digits. Even the rational half of RT, for smooth numbers instead
   474	  of primes, is far beyond them. Also, by Prop RB, statistics of smooth numbers cannot replace it.
   475	
   476	---
